The test suite is a single file — `AcceptanceTest` — and it is the held-out target for the upcoming feature: it fails now (baseline showed the NPE on the missing `checkDigit` field) and stays red until `getCheckDigit` is implemented, which I must not do here. There are no other tests, so nothing was previously green to break. The full `test` phase (main + test sources) compiles cleanly with my change (exit 0).

## What I did

The upcoming change adds `Owner::getCheckDigit` — a Luhn check digit over the digits of `customerCode`. Implemented directly, that getter would have to carry the whole Luhn loop inline, which is the flagged complexity.

This codebase already has a clear idiom for exactly this shape: a thin `@Transient` derived getter delegating its algorithm to a private static helper — `getLocality()` → `regionForPostcode(...)`. So I extracted the seam ahead of the change:

- Added a private static `luhnCheckDigit(String)` helper to `Owner` (`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`), documented in the same house style as `regionForPostcode`, placed right after the `customerCode` accessors where the future `getCheckDigit` getter will naturally sit next to it.

Its semantics match the reference `luhn(...)` in `AcceptanceBase` exactly (right-to-left, double every second digit, ignore non-digits, `0` for absent/digit-free input), so the feature will land as a trivial delegator:

```java
@Transient
public Integer getCheckDigit() {
    return luhnCheckDigit(this.customerCode);
}
```

No existing behaviour changed — the helper is a new, uncalled seam (idiomatic for a pre-change preparation), and nothing else in the class or wiring was touched. The remaining feature work (the getter, the `checkDigit` field in `openapi.yml`, and MapStruct auto-mapping) is deliberately left for the change itself.

